  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n- Shower chances tonight with warm and breezy weather Saturday\n\nWe continue to expect a short window for any one location of the \nthreat of a shower or two from mid-evening into the overnight hours \ntonight. Regional satellite and radar imagery shows the active \nfrontal zone to our SW this afternoon near the Mississippi River \nValley near the MN/IA/WI border. \n\nThere is lightning currently associated with the rain out there, but \nwe remain confident that the threat of lightning here will be \nisolated at most, and not even enough to mention at this time. Model \nprogs show the sfc based and elevated instability expected to \ndiminish toward evening, and ride almost due north, staying west of \nour area. We may see a 200 J/kg of MU CAPE and LI's both sfc based \nand aloft staying above 0c. \n\nSome of the rain is likely to make it to the ground, but that will \nbe mainly under the heavier showers with moisture quite limited \nbelow 6k ft. All of this should move out of the area prior to \ndaybreak on Saturday as the warm front passes by, and the nose of \nthe low level jet moves out. \n\nThis will set the entire area up for a warm October day with plenty \nof sunshine and breezy conditions.\n\n- Very little rain expected for the area with the remnants of Isaias \n\nClouds on the leading edge of what is left of Isaias will stream in \nSaturday night from the SSE. By 12z Sunday, some rain will lift up \ninto the area, mainly SE of Grand Rapids. The SE trend has continued \neven a little bit more over the last 24 hours, due to the logjam of \nsystems to the NW. What rain there will be will be light on amounts, \nwith most of the area that sees rain staying under a tenth of an \ninch. Of course some locally higher amounts will be possible.\n\nThe clouds and rain will move out by Monday, as the upper trough out \nwest, and upper ridge immediately west of our area pushes over the \narea. Monday is shaping up to be another warm day like Saturday with \na good warm flow from the SW moving in ahead of the next front.\n\n- Unsettled weather likely Tue and beyond with a strong storm system\n\nMonday may be the last nice fall day for a few days, as we are \nexpected to become under the influence of a much more active and \nunsettled pattern for Tuesday and beyond. \n\nThe large long wave trough out west will be trying to push east once \nIsaias moves out of the way this weekend. Strong ridging over the \nsouthern states will hold back the southern part of the ridge, while \nenergy flattens out the top of the ridge a bit. This will allow a \ncold front to sink south over the area on Tuesday. Moisture is \ninitially a bit limited as the front moves through. The front stalls \nout, and additional short waves are expected to ride along this. \nThese waves will help produce showers and storms with surges of low \nlevel moisture riding over the front ahead of the waves. \n\nThe long wave trough/low that closes off over the SW U.S. is \nforecast to lift out into the Plains by Thursday. Strong large scale \nforcing ahead of this trough will help to develop a stronger fall \nsystem and lift northward west of our area. If this holds, we would \nsee a strong surge of warm air around Thursday, with lots of Gulf \nmoisture that would bring plenty showers and storms to the area. One \ncan not rule out a severe weather event next Thu if things don't \nchange much with the low going negatively tilted over time.\n\nThe front would then go through around the Friday time frame, and \nbring in much colder air with stronger winds. The northern branch of \nthe upper jet would sink south of our area, and bring a potential \nfor lake effect rain showers.",
